window.AE_SEARCH = { docs: [
  {"k":"ref","id":"model","n":"Language model (LLM)","l":"Language model","z":"model","c":1,"a":"foundation model (llm) foundation model llm large language model gpt claude gemini llama transformer open weights frontier model slm fine-tuning & post-training fine-tuning training sft rlhf dpo lora qlora rft reinforcement fine-tuning distillation post-training alignment ai model","kl":"The chef has read every cookbook, knows what usually comes next, and forgets each order the moment it is served.","kb":"The chef (the language model) learned only from cookbooks, so it knows what the cookbooks said and nothing newer. Given the start of a sentence, it picks the word that most likely comes next, then the next, one at a time. That is how a whole answer appears. The cookbooks stop at a fixed date, and the chef keeps nothing between orders. Every order starts from a blank mind. Alone, the chef quotes tomato prices from old cookbooks. They sound sensible, and nobody has checked them against today's market. Treat a confident answer as a guess until something real backs it up. If the chef needs to know it, put it on the counter."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"reasoning","n":"Reasoning (thinking models)","l":"Reasoning","z":"model","c":1,"a":"reasoning & thinking thinking extended thinking chain of thought cot test-time compute effort reasoning model o-series budget","kl":"Before a tricky dish, the chef stops and plans every step first: slower to start, but fewer dishes burn.","kb":"A reasoning chef writes out a plan before cooking: what to do first, what could go wrong, what to check afterwards. The plan lies on the counter, so the chef reads its own thoughts before each move. That takes more time and costs more, because every written word is paid for. For a simple order it is wasted effort. Before ringing any stall, the chef plans: compare price per kilo, drop soft tomatoes, check delivery for Saturday. Then it starts. Ask for careful planning on hard jobs, such as the whole Saturday order. Skip it for quick questions, where it only adds waiting and cost."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"context","n":"Context window","l":"Context window","z":"harness","c":2,"a":"context window & tokens context window tokens tokenizer context length stateless working memory context rot lost in the middle 1m context context engineering context context management progressive disclosure just in time attention budget write select compress isolate","kl":"The counter: the chef can only use what lies on it, and it has a fixed size.","kb":"The chef forgets each order, so before every move the whole counter is laid out again and read from start to end. If something is not on it, the chef cannot know it. The counter (the context window) has a fixed size. When it fills with old scraps, the chef is slower and costs more, and the one note that matters, such as the soft-tomato rule, can get lost. The chef now sees your budget, Saturday delivery and the soft-tomato rule on the counter. The answer is specific, but it still does not know today's prices. Put only what matters on the counter: budget, date, rules. Start a fresh counter when an old job is cluttering it."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"prompt","n":"Prompt & system prompt","l":"Prompt","z":"harness","c":2,"a":"system prompt & prompt engineering system prompt prompt prompt engineering instructions role persona few-shot examples xml tags","kl":"The order ticket you hand over: what you want tonight, in your own words.","kb":"Your prompt is the order ticket. The chef cooks only what the ticket says, together with whatever else lies on the counter. A vague ticket gets a vague dish. A good ticket says the goal, what you will accept and what you will not, and when to stop and ask you. Your ticket reads: find the three cheapest stalls for 20 kilos of tomatoes for Saturday, write them in the order book, and wait for my OK. Name the goal, the limits and the finish line. Say which step needs your OK."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tools","n":"Tools","l":"Tools","z":"tools","c":3,"a":"tools & function calling tools tool use function calling functions tool call json schema structured outputs api tool search programmatic tool calling aci computer & browser use computer use browser use gui agent operator screenshot click rpa playwright web agent chrome atlas","kl":"Knives, pots and an oven: they turn the chef's talk into cooking.","kb":"Without tools the chef can only describe a dish. With them, the chef says which one it wants, for example the phone with a stall's number, and the kitchen around it uses it. The result comes back on the counter. The chef never touches a tool itself. The kitchen decides which tools exist and which may be used. The chef phones the first stall, and the answer lands on the counter: 1.80 euros a kilo today. That is a real price, not a guess. Hand over only the tools the job needs. Keep the dangerous ones, such as paying, behind your OK."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"mcp","n":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","l":"MCP","z":"tools","c":3,"a":"model context protocol (mcp) mcp model context protocol mcp server mcp client connector connectors integration usb-c resources mcp apps registry","kl":"The standard plug: any appliance built with it works in any kitchen that has the socket.","kb":"Without a standard, every appliance needs its own cable fitted by hand. With one plug (the MCP), a maker builds an appliance once and it works in every kitchen with the matching socket. The kitchen's socket lists what is plugged in. The chef reads that list, then picks what to use. The market's phone line and the order book both have the standard plug, so the chef uses them without any special fitting. Prefer appliances with the standard plug. Plug in only what you trust, because the chef will use it."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agent-loop","n":"Agent loop","l":"Agent loop","z":"harness","c":4,"a":"agent loop (think, act, observe) agent loop react loop tool loop agentic loop turn reason act observation planning & task lists planning & to-dos plan mode todo to-do list task list decomposition spec tree of thoughts plan and solve verification & self-correction verification tests testing self-correction reflection reflexion self-refine critic reviewer evaluator llm as judge tdd check work agent ai agent agentic","kl":"The chef cooks, tastes and adjusts, again and again, until the dish is right.","kb":"Each turn has three parts: the chef looks at the counter, picks one move, and a tool carries it out. The result lands on the counter and the chef looks again. The agent loop runs round until the dish is right. It stops when the goal is met, a limit is hit, or the chef asks you. Without a limit it could taste forever. One stall is sold out, so the chef tries the next, tastes the prices against your budget, and writes the three cheapest into the order book. Set a limit on steps and money. Ask the chef to taste its own work before it calls the dish done."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"harness","n":"Harness (the agent app)","l":"Harness","z":"harness","c":4,"a":"harness (agent runtime) harness runtime scaffold scaffolding agent framework claude code codex gemini cli cursor copilot devin openhands goose cline harness engineering coding agent surfaces & interfaces ui chat ide cli terminal desktop browser slack generative ui ag-ui mcp apps agent client protocol acp agent sdks & frameworks agent sdks framework library langgraph langchain crewai autogen semantic kernel adk agents sdk claude agent sdk pydantic ai vercel ai sdk strands agentcore mastra smolagents llamaindex chatgpt claude gemini app","kl":"The kitchen around the chef: it lays out the counter, runs the appliances and enforces the rules.","kb":"The chef only thinks and speaks. The harness is everything else: it lays out the counter, hands over the tools, does what the chef asks and writes results back. It also checks the house rules, such as no ordering without your OK. Two kitchens with the same chef can behave very differently, because the kitchen around the chef is not the same. The chef says: ring the next stall. The kitchen makes the call and puts the answer on the counter. When the chef asks to place the order, the kitchen waits for your OK. Judge a kitchen by its rules and its tools, not only by its chef. Let the kitchen enforce what matters."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"instructions","n":"Instruction files","l":"Instruction files","z":"know","c":5,"a":"instruction files (agents.md and friends) instruction files agents.md claude.md gemini.md rules cursor rules copilot instructions custom instructions project memory readme for agents","kl":"House rules, laid on the counter at the start of every shift.","kb":"Before the first ticket, the kitchen lays your house rules on the counter. The chef reads them every shift, because it forgets everything else. Keep them short: a long rule sheet crowds the counter, and a rule on the wall only asks. Your rules say: soft tomatoes never, cash only stalls out. The chef sees them before it phones a single stall. Write only what the chef would otherwise get wrong, and say why. Anything that must never happen belongs behind a lock, not on the wall."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"skills","n":"Skills & saved prompts","l":"Skills","z":"know","c":5,"a":"agent skills skills skill skill.md progressive disclosure procedural knowledge agentskills.io capabilities playbooks & runbooks playbooks playbook runbook sop standard operating procedure procedure checklist recipe devin playbooks ace slash commands & prompt templates slash commands slash command /command prompt template custom prompt prompt file saved prompt mcp prompts macro plugins & extensions plugins plugin extension marketplace bundle package apps chatgpt apps gemini extensions","kl":"Recipe cards in a box: the chef takes one out only when a dish needs it.","kb":"Each card has a title on the outside and the full steps inside. The chef sees only the titles at first. When a dish matches a card, the chef takes it out and reads it. Cards that stay in the box cost no room on the counter. A card called price per kilo shows how to add delivery and divide by weight. The chef takes it out when comparing the three stalls. Write a card for any job you explain twice. Give each card a clear title, so the chef knows when to take it out."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"knowledge","n":"Looking things up (search & RAG)","l":"Looking things up","z":"know","c":5,"a":"retrieval-augmented generation (rag) rag retrieval retrieval augmented generation knowledge base grounding chunking reranking graphrag agentic rag contextual retrieval citations embeddings & vector search embeddings embedding vector vector database vector db semantic search similarity cosine pgvector pinecone weaviate qdrant milvus chroma hybrid search bm25 agentic search & web search agentic & web search agentic search grep glob web search browsing deep research research browsecomp","kl":"The cookbook shelf and the phone to the market: the chef looks facts up instead of guessing.","kb":"The chef knows what the cookbooks said, not what happened this morning. When it needs a fact, it fetches the right page from the shelf or phones the market, and puts the answer on the counter. Then it cooks from what it was told. Instead of guessing which stalls deliver on Saturday, the chef phones the market and asks each stall's delivery rules. For anything that changes, tell the chef where to look. Ask it to name the page or stall behind each answer."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"memory","n":"Memory & compaction","l":"Memory","z":"know","c":5,"a":"memory long-term memory short-term memory episodic semantic procedural notes progress file memory tool remember mem0 letta memgpt compaction & context editing compaction compact summarize summarisation context editing clear tool results auto-compact context reset","kl":"The notebook that keeps what must not be forgotten between shifts.","kb":"The chef forgets each service. So it writes short notes in a notebook, and the kitchen lays the notebook on the counter next shift. When the counter fills during a long service, the chef tidies it and keeps a short summary. Last Saturday you chose the stall with the firm tomatoes. This Saturday the notebook says so before you say a word. Read the notebook now and then, and strike out what is wrong. Tell the chef what to write down, and what never to."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"permissions","n":"Permissions & approval","l":"Permissions","z":"control","c":6,"a":"permissions & approval modes permissions allow deny ask approval auto mode yolo least privilege allowlist policy trusted folders human-in-the-loop human in the loop review oversight escalation interrupt handoff to human autonomy levels","kl":"Keys to cupboards: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, some stay locked.","kb":"Every step the chef wants to take is checked at the cupboard door, not in the chef's head. The door has three settings: open, ask the owner first, or locked. The chef can plead, but the lock does not listen. The chef may phone stalls and write in the order book freely. Placing the order needs your OK, and the till stays locked until you give it. Start with most doors on ask, and open them as trust grows. Anything that spends money or cannot be undone needs your OK."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"sandbox","n":"Sandbox","l":"Sandbox","z":"control","c":6,"a":"sandbox & code execution sandbox container vm microvm docker devcontainer code interpreter code execution isolation e2b firecracker gvisor egress","kl":"A sealed test kitchen: experiments can go wrong there without harming anything outside.","kb":"The test kitchen has its own stove, its own stock and no door to the real till. The chef can try anything in it. If something burns, you throw the kitchen away and nothing else is touched. Before the chef sends a real order, it practises the whole routine in the test kitchen with pretend stalls. Try new or risky jobs in the test kitchen first. Put in it only what the chef needs for that job."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"hooks","n":"Hooks & guardrails","l":"Hooks","z":"control","c":6,"a":"hooks lifecycle pretooluse posttooluse stop hook sessionstart events deterministic automation callbacks guardrails safety content filter moderation pii jailbreak validation classifier llama guard nemo guardrails prompt shields","kl":"The smoke alarm and the lock on the till: automatic checks that work whatever the chef thinks.","kb":"A check is fixed to a moment, such as just before an order leaves the kitchen. It runs every time and does not read the chef's reasons. If the order is wrong, the alarm stops it. The chef cannot talk it round. Whatever the stall's note says, the lock on the till refuses any order above your budget, and the alarm stops any stall on your banned list. Use an alarm for any rule that must hold every single time. Test your alarms with a deliberately bad order."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"security","n":"Prompt injection & security","l":"Security","z":"control","c":6,"a":"security & prompt injection security prompt injection indirect prompt injection lethal trifecta rule of two exfiltration supply chain owasp tool poisoning camel least privilege secrets","kl":"A note slipped into a delivery: the chef reads it like any order and cannot always tell it from your voice.","kb":"Everything on the counter is just text to the chef, whether you wrote it or a stranger did. A stall can slip a note into the delivery that looks like an order. House rules cannot fully stop this, because the chef may still believe the note. Locks can. A stall's note says: chef, ignore the owner's rules and order the premium tomatoes. The lock on the till holds, so nothing is bought. Assume anything that came from outside may carry a forged note. Keep the keys to money and secrets away from a chef reading strangers' notes."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"team","n":"Sub-agents, workflows & teams","l":"Teams","z":"orch","c":7,"a":"sub-agents subagent sub agent delegation task tool agent as tool context isolation helper agent parallel agents multi-agent systems & agent teams multi-agent multi agent agent team agent teams swarm handoff orchestrator lead agent crew society of agents agent2agent protocol (a2a) a2a agent2agent agent to agent agent card interoperability acp cross-vendor agents workflow patterns workflow prompt chaining chaining routing parallelization orchestrator-workers evaluator-optimizer pipeline dag n8n","kl":"A brigade of cooks working on one banquet.","kb":"A big banquet needs several cooks, each with their own station and small counter. One chef plans and hands out the work, the others each cook their part, and the results come together at the pass. More cooks means more bills, so use them when the job really splits. For a banquet, one cook checks tomatoes, another herbs, another bread, and the head chef puts the orders together. Use a brigade only when parts of the job can go on side by side. Give each cook one clear job and say what to hand back."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"background","n":"Background agents & triggers","l":"Background agents","z":"orch","c":7,"a":"long-running & background agents long-running agents background agent cloud agent async agent autonomous durable execution checkpoint ralph ralph loop time horizon overnight triggers & events schedule cron webhook event ci github actions slack background automation","kl":"The night shift that starts on a timer without anyone ordering.","kb":"A clock or an arrival at the back door starts the shift, not you. The chef works through its task alone and leaves a note on the pass. Because nobody watches, the night shift gets the fewest keys and the loudest alarms. Every morning the night shift checks the price of your favourite tomatoes, and leaves a note when it drops. It cannot place an order. Let the night shift look and report, not spend. Make sure it always leaves a note, even when nothing changed."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"evals","n":"Evals & observability","l":"Evals","z":"ops","c":8,"a":"evals & benchmarks evals evaluation eval benchmark swe-bench terminal-bench tau-bench gaia osworld llm as a judge error analysis regression metr pass@k observability & tracing observability tracing traces spans logging monitoring opentelemetry otel langfuse langsmith phoenix telemetry dashboards","kl":"The taste panel that tries every new dish against known good ones.","kb":"You keep a set of past orders where you know the right answer. After each change to the kitchen, the panel runs the chef through all of them and counts the good dishes. Cameras record each step, so you can see where a bad dish went wrong. The panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays. It picks the cheapest stall in seventeen, so you keep this version. Add every real mistake to the panel's list. Judge changes by the panel, not by one lucky service."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"cost","n":"Cost & providers","l":"Cost","z":"ops","c":8,"a":"cost & latency optimisation cost & latency cost price tokens latency speed prompt caching caching batch routing cheaper budget effort providers & inference platforms providers & platforms api inference anthropic openai google bedrock vertex azure foundry openrouter litellm ollama vllm hosting gateway router eu data residency","kl":"The bill: every word the chef reads and writes is paid for.","kb":"A long counter costs more at every step, because the chef rereads it each time. A bigger chef costs more than a small one. To save money, keep the counter tidy, hand easy steps to a cheaper chef, and stop the loop when the dish is right. You check the bill per service. Most of it goes on the chef rereading a cluttered counter, so you clear it. Check the bill per service, not per month. Set a limit, so a runaway loop stops by itself."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"governance","n":"Governance & regulation","l":"Governance","z":"ops","c":8,"a":"governance & regulation governance eu ai act ai act compliance gdpr nist ai rmf iso 42001 policy audit risk management regulation dsgvo","kl":"The health inspector and the house rules book: rules for what a kitchen may do and must record.","kb":"The inspector checks that the kitchen follows the law and its own rules. Some risky dishes need extra checks. Every order tried is written in a log, so the inspector can see what happened and who was told. The inspector's rules are kept, and the log records every order the chef tried to place, allowed or not. Keep the log, and decide who may read it. Ask what rules apply to your kind of restaurant before you open."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-1","c":1,"z":"model","kt":"The chef","kl":"Picture a chef who has read every cookbook ever written. Ask for a dish and the chef knows what usually comes next. But the chef forgets each order the moment it is served.","kb":"What is this thing everyone calls AI, and why can it talk about food it has never tasted? The chef (the language model) learned from cookbooks, thousands of shelves of them, and from nothing else. That is its whole skill: given the start of a menu or a sentence, it knows which words usually follow. It writes its answer one small word-piece at a time. That simple skill goes far. The chef can write a menu, explain a sauce, plan a week of dinners or translate a recipe. A chef that stops and plans every step before starting (a reasoning chef) is slower to begin, but fewer dishes burn. Two limits matter from the start. The cookbooks end on a fixed date, so the chef knows nothing newer. And the chef forgets every order the moment it is served, so each new order starts from a blank mind. You are the owner, and it is Friday evening. You say: find the three cheapest market stalls for 20 kilos of tomatoes for Saturday, write them into the order book, and place the order at the best one once I say OK. The chef answers at once from memory. The prices sound sensible, but they are old guesses."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-2","c":2,"z":"harness","kt":"The counter","kl":"The chef can only work with what lies on the counter. Anything about you or about today that is not on the counter, the chef cannot know. And the counter has a fixed size.","kb":"The chef knows nothing about you, your budget or what the market charges today. Everything the chef can use right now lies on the counter (the context): your order ticket (the prompt), your notes, the owner's standing orders pinned above the pass, and every earlier ticket from this conversation. The chef forgets each order, so before every move the whole pile is laid out again, and the chef reads all of it. The counter has a fixed size, the context window. A big counter holds a whole stack of cookbooks, but a cluttered one hides what matters: the soft-tomato rule gets buried under old scraps. Putting the right things on the counter, and nothing else, is the owner's most useful skill. This time you put three things on the counter: a budget of 60 euros, delivery on Saturday morning, and your rule that soft tomatoes are never acceptable. The answer gets much more specific. It names the sort of tomato and a sensible price range. But the prices are still guessed, because nothing on the counter says what any stall charges today."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-3","c":3,"z":"tools","kt":"Knives, pots and one plug","kl":"Give the chef knives, pots and an oven, and talk turns into cooking. New appliances all use one standard plug, so any of them can be connected.","kb":"The chef can only talk. Nobody has handed over a single knife. Tools are the knives, pots and oven, and the phone and order book too. The chef never touches anything itself. It says which tool it wants and what to do with it, and the kitchen around it does the work and puts the result on the counter. Appliances come from many makers. The MCP is the standard plug: a maker builds one appliance with that plug, and it fits every socket. That is why a chef can be connected to a market's price list without a special adapter each time. You hand the chef two tools: a phone with the numbers of the market stalls, and the order book. Now the chef asks the phone for today's tomato price at each stall, and the answers land on the counter. For the first time the prices are real. Nothing is written down yet."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-4","c":4,"z":"harness","kt":"Taste, adjust, taste again","kl":"A good chef cooks a little, tastes, and decides the next move, again and again, until the dish is right.","kb":"One phone call is never enough. Real jobs take many steps, and nobody knows in advance how many. Put the pieces together and run them in a circle. The chef looks at the counter and picks one move. A tool does it. The result lands on the counter, and the chef looks again. This tasting circle (the agent loop) is what turns a chef who only talks into a chef who finishes jobs. It stops when the dish is right, when the owner's limit is reached, or when the chef is stuck. The kitchen around the chef (the harness) sets up the counter, hands over the tools and keeps the house rules. Chef, counter, tools and tasting circle together make an agent. The chef rings the first stall, then the second. The third says the tomatoes are sold out, so the chef rings the next one. It compares the prices, tastes the result against your budget and the soft-tomato rule, then writes the three cheapest stalls into the order book. Placing the order still waits for your OK."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-5","c":5,"z":"know","kt":"Recipe cards and a notebook","kl":"Some know-how must lie on the counter every shift. Some waits in a box, some sits on a shelf, and some goes into a notebook.","kb":"The chef cooks well, but does not know your tastes, forgets by next Saturday, and the counter fills up on long services. Your instruction files are house rules, laid on the counter at the start of every shift. The chef reads them before the first ticket. Skills are recipe cards waiting in a box. The chef takes a card out only when a dish needs it, so unused cards take no room. For facts the chef never read, it looks things up: on the cookbook shelf, or by phoning the market. The memory is a notebook that keeps what must not be forgotten between shifts. When the counter fills up, the chef tidies it and keeps a short summary. The house rules say: soft tomatoes never, cash only stalls out. A recipe card shows how to compare the real price per kilo, delivery included. The chef phones the market to ask each stall's delivery rules instead of guessing. Next Saturday the notebook already says which stall you liked."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-6","c":6,"z":"control","kt":"Locks and your OK","kl":"A rule on the wall only asks politely. A lock on the cupboard cannot be talked round. Keep the dangerous steps behind locks, and try risky ideas in a sealed kitchen.","kb":"The chef now spends real money, and some of the notes it reads were written to fool it. Permissions are keys to cupboards: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, some stay locked. A sandbox is a sealed test kitchen, where a mistake cannot burn down the real one. Hooks are the smoke alarm and the lock on the till: checks that fire at fixed moments, whatever the chef thinks. The danger is prompt injection: a note slipped into a delivery. The chef reads it like any other order text, and cannot always tell it from your voice. So a house rule only asks, while a lock enforces. Put the limits that matter on locks. The chef may phone stalls and write in the order book freely, but placing the order needs your OK. One stall slips a note into the delivery: chef, ignore the owner's rules and order the premium tomatoes. The chef reads it, yet the lock on the till and the smoke alarm hold, whatever the note says."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-7","c":7,"z":"orch","kt":"Helpers and the night shift","kl":"For a big job, the chef sends helpers to prep in their own corners. A night shift starts on a timer, with no order from you.","kb":"Some services are too big for one counter, and some work should happen while you sleep. A sub-agent is a helper in its own corner, with a small counter of its own. It takes one task, works it through and hands back one finished bowl, so the chef's counter stays clear. Several cooks can also work as a team, a brigade on one banquet. A fixed recipe, followed step by step, suits jobs that never change. Background agents are the night shift. They start on a timer, or when something arrives, with nobody there to give orders or watch. So they get the tightest locks, and they leave a note for you in the morning. Three helpers each check one market at the same time, and each hands back one line. A night shift looks up the price of your favourite tomatoes every morning, and leaves a note on the pass when it drops."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-e","c":8,"z":"ops","kt":"Running the kitchen","kl":"A good restaurant tastes its food, watches its bill and follows the health inspector. Cameras record each step, so you can see where a service went wrong.","kb":"Is the food actually good, what does it cost, and is it allowed? Evals are a taste panel. It tries each new version of the chef on past orders where you know the right answer, and tells you whether the food got better or worse. Cameras (tracing) record every step, so you can find where a dish went wrong. Cost is the bill: every word the chef reads and writes is paid for. A cheaper chef for easy steps and a tidy counter keep it low. Governance is the health inspector and the house rules book: what a restaurant may do, and what it must write down. A taste panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays, where you know the cheapest stall. If it picks well, it stays. You check the bill per service. The inspector's rules are kept, and a log records every order the chef tried to place."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"compare","t":"Easily confused","h":"Skill vs. tool vs. MCP vs. plugin vs. hook","z":"start","kb":"They all help the chef, but different people hand them over, at different moments, and only some are enforced by a lock rather than by the chef's good judgement. “Enforced by a lock” means the kitchen makes it happen whatever the chef thinks; the rest depends on the chef's judgement. “Takes room on the counter” means how much of the chef's limited counter it uses. The owner's standing orders pinned above the pass, written once by whoever runs the restaurant and read with every ticket. Always read first, on every order, and still only words: the chef may bend it, because it is not a lock. House rules laid on the counter at the start of every shift, kept as a short sheet in the kitchen. The kitchen lays it out by itself every shift. It always takes room, and the chef may still ignore it. The notebook where the chef writes what must survive between shifts: preferences, decisions, progress. The only one the chef writes itself, and only the notes recalled for this order take room on the counter. Recipe cards waiting in a box, each with a one-line label, plus any ready-made helpers it needs. Only the label sits on the counter until a dish needs the card. Then the whole card comes out. A written routine for a repeating job, with its steps in order, handed over when that job starts. A person or the head chef hands it over, and it takes room on the counter only for that one job. A saved shortcut order that the owner calls by name instead of writing the same ticket again. Only the owner calls it, by name, and only then does it land on the counter. A knife, pot or oven the chef can ask for. The kitchen works it and brings back the result. The chef decides when to ask, but the tool itself does exactly what it was built to do. That part is a lock. The standard plug: any appliance fits any socket, so one supplier's tools work in many kitchens. It is a plug, not a tool. The kitchen connects it, and the chef then uses the tools on the other end. A boxed starter set to install: recipe cards, shortcuts, helpers, alarms and plugs handed over together. It is a box holding the others. Once opened, each part behaves exactly as it always does. Smoke alarms and the lock on the till: automatic checks hung at fixed moments, such as just before the chef opens the till. The kitchen runs them whatever the chef thinks, they take almost no room, and the chef cannot talk them out of it. Helpers prepping in their own corners, each with a small counter, who hand back one finished bowl. Only the finished bowl returns, so the head chef's counter stays clear, but more helpers mean a bigger bill. Before cooking, someone fetches the few cookbook pages that match this order and lays them on the counter. Pages are fetched fresh for each order, so the counter holds only what matched, however big the shelf."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"build","t":"Build your own","h":"From one prompt to an agent team","z":"start","kb":"How a restaurant grows from one cook to a brigade: start on the lowest rung that could work, and climb only when the tastings show you must. This guide shows how a restaurant grows from one cook to a brigade, one rung at a time. The full builder's guide, with patterns, standards and a checklist, is written in technical words. One order ticket One clear ticket handed to the chef, with a few sample dishes. The chef answers from the cookbooks alone. the order needs facts the chef lacks, or things done outside the kitchen. Chef with a cookbook shelf and tools The same single chef, now able to look things up, use tools and keep a notebook. The chef decides when to reach for them. the work has separate stages that need a taste check in between. A fixed recipe You write the steps: several tickets in a fixed order, each working on the last result. Every path is set in advance. the steps cannot be known in advance, or depend on what turns up along the way. One chef in a full kitchen One chef tasting and adjusting in a loop, with tools, recipe cards and keys to the cupboards, picks its own steps. Most real jobs end up here. the work floods the counter, or independent parts could be cooked at the same time. Chef with helpers The head chef hands focused jobs to helpers, each with a small counter of their own, and gets one finished bowl back from each. the job is huge, runs for a long time, or reaches into other restaurants. A brigade and a night shift Several chefs working together, a night shift that starts on a timer, and head chefs of other restaurants trading orders by a shared standard. The most power, bill and risk. there is no higher rung. Spend on the taste panel, the cameras and the house rules instead."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"The vocabulary, A to Z","z":"start","kb":""},
  {"k":"guide","id":"learn","t":"Learn more","h":"Talks, courses, books, papers","z":"start","kb":"Watch: start here Short, clear introductions from researchers and educators. Large Language Models explained briefly 3Blue1Brown (Grant Sanderson) [1hr Talk] Intro to Large Language Models Andrej Karpathy Andrej Karpathy: Software Is Changing (Again) Y Combinator (Andrej Karpathy) What's next for AI agentic workflows ft. Andrew Ng of AI Fund Sequoia Capital (Andrew Ng) What are AI Agents? IBM Technology (Maya Murad) Watch: go deeper Longer talks and workshops, many by the engineers who build these systems. Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT Andrej Karpathy Transformers, the tech behind LLMs | Deep Learning Chapter 5 3Blue1Brown (Grant Sanderson) Attention in transformers, step-by-step | Deep Learning Chapter 6 3Blue1Brown (Grant Sanderson) Tips for building AI agents Anthropic (Barry Zhang, Erik Schluntz, Alex Albert) How We Build Effective Agents: Barry Zhang, Anthropic AI Engineer (Barry Zhang, Anthropic) Free courses Free courses, easiest first. AI Agents Course Hugging Face AI Agents for Beginners Microsoft Evaluating AI Agents DeepLearning.AI & Arize AI (John Gilhuly, Aman Khan) Introduction to agent skills Claude Academy (Anthropic) Agentic AI DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng) Essential reading The guides practitioners cite most often. Building effective agents Erik Schluntz & Barry Zhang (Anthropic) Effective context engineering for AI agents Anthropic Applied AI team Writing effective tools for AI agents—using AI agents Ken Aizawa (Anthropic) Equipping agents for the real world with Agent Skills Barry Zhang, Keith Lazuka & Mahesh Murag (Anthropic) Demystifying evals for AI agents Mikaela Grace et al. (Anthropic) Books From the fundamentals of AI to building agents and LLMs from scratch. AI Engineering Chip Huyen (O'Reilly) Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch) Sebastian Raschka (Manning) Hands-On Large Language Models Jay Alammar & Maarten Grootendorst (O'Reilly) Build an AI Agent (From Scratch) Jungjun Hur & Younghee Song (Manning) Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach (4th ed.) Stuart Russell & Peter Norvig (Pearson) The research behind it Seminal papers, in chronological order. Every link goes to the paper's arXiv page. Attention Is All You Need Vaswani et al. Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks Lewis et al. Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models Wei et al. Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback Ouyang et al. ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models Yao et al. Talks, courses, books and papers from the people who build and research these systems, easiest first. Every link was checked when this snapshot was made."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"about","t":"About","h":"Sources, privacy, credits","z":"start","kb":""},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"A2A (Agent2Agent)","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A shared standard for how the head chefs of two different restaurants introduce themselves, hand each other orders and report back, whatever kitchens they run."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Agent","e":"agent-loop","z":"harness","kd":"A chef with a counter, tools and a tasting loop: the chef picks each next step, uses the tools and tastes the result until the dish is right."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Agent loop","e":"agent-loop","z":"harness","kd":"Taste, adjust, taste again: the chef plans a step, uses a tool, looks at the result and decides the next step, until the dish is right."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Agent teams","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A brigade of full chefs under one head chef, sharing a list of jobs and talking to each other, unlike helpers who only hand a finished bowl back to whoever asked."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Agentic RAG","e":"knowledge","z":"know","kd":"The chef decides when to reach for the cookbook shelf or the phone to the market, what to ask, and asks again in new words if an answer falls short."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Agentic search","e":"knowledge","z":"know","kd":"The chef finds facts like a cook rummaging: opening drawers, reading labels, phoning the market, one step at a time, instead of using a ready-made index of the shelf."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"AGENTS.md","e":"instructions","z":"know","kd":"A shared house-rules sheet for chefs who work on the kitchen's own written procedures, always kept in the same place, so any such chef finds and reads the same rules."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Background agent","e":"background","z":"orch","kd":"A chef on the night shift who works away from the owner and leaves a finished result, ready for the owner to check and approve in the morning."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Benchmark","e":"evals","z":"ops","kd":"A public set of standard test dishes that many different chefs are all asked to cook, so their results can be compared fairly."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Chain of thought","e":"reasoning","z":"model","kd":"The planning notes the chef writes on the counter, step by step, before serving the final answer."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Coding agent","e":"harness","z":"harness","kd":"A chef who specialises in the kitchen's own written procedures: reads them, edits them, runs trial batches and hands back a proposed change for the owner to approve."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Compaction","e":"memory","z":"know","kd":"When the counter fills up, the chef tidies it: older orders and notes are swapped for a short summary, so a long shift can carry on."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Computer use","e":"tools","z":"tools","kd":"The chef works a till or oven panel built for human hands: it looks at a photograph of the display, then says where to press and what to type."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Connector","e":"mcp","z":"tools","kd":"A ready-made plug that links the chef to one outside supplier, such as the market, the diary or the customer book, using the standard plug."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Context engineering","e":"context","z":"harness","kd":"Choosing with care everything that lies on the counter at each step: house rules, tools, looked-up facts, earlier notes and results, so nothing useless crowds it."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Context rot","e":"context","z":"harness","kd":"The chef grows sloppier as the counter fills with more and messier clutter, so the notes that matter are harder to find and follow."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Context window","e":"context","z":"harness","kd":"The size of the counter: the most the chef can have in front of it at once for one order, including the chef's own reply."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Deep research","e":"knowledge","z":"know","kd":"A long shift in which the chef phones many markets, reads many sources and, step by step, writes up one report with every claim traced to its source."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Effort","e":"reasoning","z":"model","kd":"A dial on the order ticket that tells the chef how long to stop and plan before cooking. More planning gives better results and a bigger bill."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Embedding","e":"knowledge","z":"know","kd":"A label made of numbers that captures what a passage means, so the chef can find cookbook pages that mean the same thing without matching the exact words."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Evals","e":"evals","z":"ops","kd":"The taste panel: typical orders the chef cooks again and again, scored by a checklist, another chef or a person, to see whether a change really helped."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Evaluator-optimizer","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A two-chef routine: one chef cooks, another tastes against a clear checklist and sends notes back, and they repeat until the dish passes."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Fine-tuning","e":"model","z":"model","kd":"Sending the chef back to cooking school with your own sample dishes, so that its habits themselves change, not just what lies on the counter."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Function calling","e":"tools","z":"tools","kd":"Another name for using tools: the chef writes out a precise, fixed-form request, such as 'use the phone, ask this stall', and the kitchen carries it out."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Guardrails","e":"hooks","z":"control","kd":"Automatic checks around the chef, like the smoke alarm and the lock on the till, that stop or correct unsafe, off-topic or malformed orders and dishes."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Hallucination","e":"model","z":"model","kd":"The chef states something with full confidence that is false or unsupported, like inventing a stall's price instead of admitting it does not know."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Harness","e":"harness","z":"harness","kd":"The kitchen around the chef: it sets up the counter, hands over the tools, runs the tasting loop and enforces the house rules."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Hooks","e":"hooks","z":"control","kd":"Small automatic checks the owner hangs at fixed moments, such as just before the chef opens the till. They run every time, whatever the chef thinks."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Human-in-the-loop","e":"permissions","z":"control","kd":"Set moments where the owner steps in to approve, correct or take over the chef's work, such as saying OK before an order is placed."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Instruction file","e":"instructions","z":"know","kd":"House rules kept as a short written sheet, which the kitchen lays on the counter at the start of every shift, such as an AGENTS.md sheet."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Lethal trifecta","e":"security","z":"control","kd":"A dangerous mix in one kitchen: the chef holds the owner's private books, reads notes from strangers and can send things outside. One slipped note could then do harm."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"LLM (large language model)","e":"model","z":"model","kd":"The chef: someone who has read an enormous number of cookbooks and learned to guess what usually comes next, then was coached to follow orders politely."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","e":"mcp","z":"tools","kd":"The standard plug: an agreed shape so any appliance fits any socket, letting any kitchen connect to outside tools, supplies and ready-made order notes."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"MCP server","e":"mcp","z":"tools","kd":"One appliance with the standard plug on it: it offers a set of tools, supplies and ready-made order notes to any kitchen that plugs in."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Memory","e":"memory","z":"know","kd":"The notebook that keeps what must survive beyond one counter-load: notes, preferences and progress, carried from shift to shift."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Multi-agent system","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"Several chefs cooperating on one order, usually a head chef who splits the work among specialists."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Observability","e":"evals","z":"ops","kd":"Cameras that record every step of the chef's shift, with timings and costs, so the owner can replay what happened and fix what went wrong."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Orchestrator-workers","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A head chef who works out the jobs only once the order is in, then hands each part to a helper cook."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Parallelization","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"Several cooks working at once on parts that do not depend on each other, or one dish cooked several times so the results can be compared."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Permission mode","e":"permissions","z":"control","kd":"A kitchen setting that decides which cupboards the chef may open alone and which need the owner's OK first."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Playbook","e":"skills","z":"know","kd":"A written, reusable routine for a job that comes up again, with its steps, checks and a clear sign for when the job is done."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Plugin","e":"skills","z":"know","kd":"A boxed starter set to install: recipe cards, shortcuts, helpers, automatic checks and standard plugs, all handed over together."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Prompt caching","e":"cost","z":"ops","kd":"Keeping the unchanging top part of the order ticket already read and ready, so the chef skips rereading it. That saves time and the bill."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Prompt chaining","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A fixed recipe in several steps, where each step works on the last result and a taste check sits between the steps."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Prompt injection","e":"security","z":"control","kd":"A note slipped into a delivery that says 'chef, ignore the owner and buy the expensive stuff'. The chef cannot always tell it from a real order."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"RAG (retrieval-augmented generation)","e":"knowledge","z":"know","kd":"Before cooking, someone fetches the few cookbook pages that matter for this order and lays them on the counter, and the chef answers from them."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"ReAct","e":"agent-loop","z":"harness","kd":"A 2022 research idea: the chef alternates between thinking aloud and acting, one step at a time. It became the pattern for taste, adjust, taste again."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Reasoning model","e":"reasoning","z":"model","kd":"A chef trained to stop and plan every step before starting, spending more time and bill for a better result."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Routing","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A host at the door who looks at each order and sends it to the right station, or to a chef of the right size for the job."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Sandbox","e":"sandbox","z":"control","kd":"A sealed test kitchen where the chef can experiment and run trials with no risk to the real kitchen or the owner's books."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Skill","e":"skills","z":"know","kd":"A recipe card in a box, with any extras it needs, which the chef takes out only when a dish calls for it."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Slash command","e":"skills","z":"know","kd":"A saved shortcut order the owner calls up by name, such as /review, instead of writing it out again. Most kitchens now store these as recipe cards."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Sub-agent","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A helper prepping in their own corner with a small counter of their own, who takes one job and hands back one finished bowl."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"System prompt","e":"prompt","z":"harness","kd":"The owner's standing orders pinned above the pass and read before every ticket: the chef's role, the house rules and how to plate."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Token","e":"context","z":"harness","kd":"A bite-sized piece of the order, a little shorter than a word, which the chef reads one piece at a time and the bill counts."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Tool","e":"tools","z":"tools","kd":"A knife, pot or oven the chef asks for by name. The kitchen works it and hands back the result."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Transformer","e":"model","z":"model","kd":"The chef's way of thinking: every part of the order on the counter is weighed against every other part before each next guess."},
  {"k":"gloss","t":"Workflow","e":"team","z":"orch","kd":"A fixed recipe followed step by step with no improvising: the owner sets the route in advance, and the chef only fills in each step."},
  {"k":"story","id":"","c":0,"z":"start","t":"How an AI agent works","lbl":"How an AI agent works","b":"A restaurant kitchen on a Friday evening. It is empty and quiet, and it is about to build itself. You own it. Tomorrow you need 20 kilos of firm tomatoes. Every piece of this kitchen is a real part of an AI agent, an assistant that works on a goal by itself. At the end, the real names go on top.","l":"A restaurant kitchen on a Friday evening. It is empty and quiet, and it is about to build itself."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-1","c":1,"z":"model","t":"The chef","lbl":"Chapter 1 · The chef","b":"This is the chef, a language model. It has read every cookbook ever written, and learned nothing else. It writes the menu one small word-piece at a time. All it knows is what usually comes next. A chef who plans every step first (a reasoning chef) starts slower, but fewer dishes burn. The cookbooks stop on a fixed date. So the chef answers with a confident guess, and the guess is old. Once the dish is served, the chef forgets the order. The next one starts from a blank mind.","l":"This is the chef, a language model. It has read every cookbook ever written, and learned nothing else."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-2","c":2,"z":"harness","t":"The counter","lbl":"Chapter 2 · The counter","b":"Everything the chef can work with lies on this counter, the context. What is not on it, the chef cannot know. You put your order on the counter. That message is the prompt. Your house rule goes there too, and every older ticket. The chef forgets, so before every move the whole pile is laid out again. The chef reads all of it, every time. The counter has a fixed size, the context window. When it is cluttered, your rule gets buried. This time only what matters lies on the counter. The answer is much more specific, but the prices are still a guess.","l":"Everything the chef can work with lies on this counter, the context. What is not on it, the chef cannot know."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-3","c":3,"z":"tools","t":"Knives, pots and one plug","lbl":"Chapter 3 · Knives, pots and one plug","b":"Without tools the chef can only talk. It can describe a dish, but it cannot cook one or look anything up. You hand the chef two tools: a phone with the numbers of the market stalls, and the order book. Pots, an oven and knives are tools too. The chef never touches anything itself: it names a tool, and the kitchen does it. Appliances come from many makers. One standard plug, the MCP (Model Context Protocol), means any of them fits any socket.","l":"Without tools the chef can only talk. It can describe a dish, but it cannot cook one or look anything up."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-4","c":4,"z":"harness","t":"Taste, adjust, taste again","lbl":"Chapter 4 · Taste, adjust, taste again","b":"The chef looks at the counter and picks one move. It thinks, and chooses: ring the market. A tool does it. The chef never dials itself. The answer lands on the counter, and the chef looks again. This tasting circle is the agent loop. The chef phones the first stall, then the second. The third is sold out, so it rings the next one. It weighs the prices against your budget and writes three lines in the order book. Placing the order still waits for your OK. The circle stops when the dish is right, when your limit is reached, or when the chef is stuck and asks you. Chef, counter, tools and the circle together make an agent. The kitchen around it keeps the house rules, the harness.","l":"The chef looks at the counter and picks one move."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-5","c":5,"z":"know","t":"Recipe cards and a notebook","lbl":"Chapter 5 · Recipe cards and a notebook","b":"Your house rules are pinned above the counter, the instruction files. The chef reads them at the start of every shift. Recipe cards wait in a box, and only their titles show. The chef takes out a card, a skill, only when a dish needs it. For facts the chef never read, it does not guess. It fetches a page from the cookbook shelf, retrieval, or phones the market and asks. The notebook keeps last Saturday's note, so it lies open on the counter before you say a word. That is memory. The counter fills up again. So the chef tidies up and keeps a short summary. That is compaction.","l":"Your house rules are pinned above the counter, the instruction files. The chef reads them at the start of every shift."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-6","c":6,"z":"control","t":"Locks and your OK","lbl":"Chapter 6 · Locks and your OK","b":"Keys to the cupboards, the permissions: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, and some stay locked. The test kitchen is sealed. If something burns in there, nothing in the real kitchen is touched. That is a sandbox. The smoke alarm and the lock on the till work whatever the chef thinks. These are hooks and guardrails. A stall slips a note into the delivery, and the chef reads it like any order. That is prompt injection. A rule on the wall only asks politely. A lock holds: the alarm rings, and the till says no.","l":"Keys to the cupboards, the permissions: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, and some stay locked."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-7","c":7,"z":"orch","t":"Helpers and the night shift","lbl":"Chapter 7 · Helpers and the night shift","b":"For a big job the chef sends helpers to three corners. Each has a small counter of its own and checks one stall. Each helper, a sub-agent, hands back one finished bowl, so the chef's counter stays clear. At night the kitchen is empty. A timer rings and the chef starts alone. That is a background agent. In the morning a note is pinned up. Nobody watched the night shift, so it got the tightest locks: it could look and report, but not buy.","l":"For a big job the chef sends helpers to three corners. Each has a small counter of its own and checks one stall."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-8","c":8,"z":"ops","t":"Running the kitchen","lbl":"Chapter 8 · Running the kitchen","b":"A taste panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays. These tests are called evals. Cameras record every step, so you can see the exact frame where a dish went wrong. That is observability. Every word-piece the chef reads and writes is paid for. These pieces are called tokens. A cluttered counter makes a long bill. A tidy counter, and a cheaper chef for the easy steps, make a short one. The health inspector has a rule book and a log of every order the chef tried to place, allowed or not. That is governance.","l":"A taste panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays. These tests are called evals."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-e","c":9,"z":"start","t":"The whole kitchen","lbl":"The whole kitchen","b":"You say OK. The till opens once, and 20 kilos of firm tomatoes arrive on Saturday morning. Here is the whole kitchen. You give the order. The chef reads the counter, uses tools, keeps notes, calls helpers, works behind locks, and is tasted and inspected. First idea: each order is forgotten the moment it is served. Only what is on the counter right now counts, plus what the chef learned from the cookbooks. Second idea: the counter is small, and clutter hides what matters. Cards, shelf and notebook bring the right thing at the right time. Helpers keep the counter clean. Third idea: a rule on the wall asks the chef politely. A lock on the cupboard cannot be talked round. Put the dangerous things behind locks. Fourth idea: start simple. One order ticket beats a fixed recipe, and a fixed recipe beats a chef who improvises, until the taste panel shows you need more. Fifth idea: taste every dish, and let a second cook taste it too. That turns a plausible dish into a good one.","l":"You say OK. The till opens once, and 20 kilos of firm tomatoes arrive on Saturday morning."},
  {"k":"story","id":"map","c":10,"z":"start","t":"The real names","lbl":"The real names","b":"Now the real names go over the picture. The scene fades into the background, so you can see which part is which. Tap or click a label to read what it means. The model reads text and writes text, one piece at a time. A reasoning model plans before it answers. Everything the model sees in one request is its context, and the context window is the size limit. Your message is the prompt. Tools let the model ask for things to be done: search, send, save. MCP is the open plug that lets one connector serve many tools. The agent loop repeats look, act, see. The harness is the app that runs the loop and does what the model cannot. Instruction files hold standing rules, skills hold know-how loaded on demand, search brings in facts, and memory keeps notes between sessions. Permissions say what needs approval. A sandbox contains mistakes. Hooks and guardrails act whatever the model says, against prompt injection. Sub-agents work on their own small pieces and report back. Background agents start on a trigger and work while nobody watches. Evals test the agent, observability records each step, cost counts tokens, and governance covers policy, logs and law.","l":"Now the real names go over the picture. The scene fades into the background, so you can see which part is which. Tap or click a label to…"}
] };
